{
  "image_name": "4bc8623505899f75a4d79e319ca2682c.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how injured liver tissue recruits and coordinates immune cells through chemokine signaling. Damaged hepatocytes release danger signals that activate Kupffer cells, the liver-resident macrophages. These Kupffer cells then produce inflammatory mediators such as IL-1 and TNF and secrete several chemokines, especially CCL2, CXCL1, CXCL2, CXCL8, and CXCL16, which diffuse toward the sinusoid.\n\nThese signals attract circulating immune cells into the liver by matching chemokines with their receptors: monocytes are drawn in mainly through CCL2–CCR2, neutrophils through CXCL1/2/8 acting on CXCR1, CXCR2, and CXCR8, and NKT cells through CXCL16–CXCR6. Once recruited, these cells appear to amplify local inflammation and interact with macrophages and Kupffer cells.\n\nThe diagram also shows stellate cells participating in this network. They respond to CCL2 via CCR2 and also produce CCL2 themselves, suggesting a positive feedback loop between stellate cells and Kupffer cells. On the right, γδ T cells produce IL-17, which seems to influence stellate-cell activity. Overall, the image depicts a chemokine-driven inflammatory circuit linking liver injury, resident liver cells, and incoming immune cells.",
